From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Guy Bedford <gbedford@cloudflare.com>
Date: Tue, 28 Jul 2026 12:48:45 -0700
Subject: [wasm] memory.discard prototype for the memory-control proposal

Port of the experimental memory.discard prototype (CL 4300676,
I71338ced369f881fc3d0091a0f7968bb89df0705) to current V8, updated to
the memory.discard sub-proposal semantics of the memory-control
proposal as prototyped in SpiderMonkey:

  memory.discard $memidx : [addr:idx size:idx] -> []
  WebAssembly.Memory.prototype.discard(byteOffset, byteLength)

Both zero the given wasm-page-aligned region and release its physical
pages back to the OS (madvise(MADV_DONTNEED) on Linux, decommit +
recommit elsewhere), trapping/throwing on unaligned or out-of-bounds
ranges. Gated behind a new standalone feature flag,
--experimental-wasm-memory-discard, independent of the existing
--wasm-memory-control mapping features.

Implemented in Liftoff and Turboshaft via a wasm::memory_discard
external reference, following the memory.fill pattern, and in the
jitless Wasm interpreter (DrumBrake) following memory.fill's handler
shape, with memory_discard whitelisted in the jitless feature mask.
All three execution paths (JS API, jitted opcode, interpreter) funnel
through BackingStore::DiscardWasmMemory as a single point for the
physical release. Supports multi-memory and memory64; shared memories
are rejected for now.

Embedder control:

- Isolate::SetWasmMemoryDiscardEnabledCallback enables memory.discard
  per-context, following the WasmCustomDescriptorsEnabledCallback
  pattern: the callback feeds WasmEnabledFeatures::FromContext
  (enabling the opcode for modules compiled in that context) and
  WasmJs::InstallConditionalFeatures installs the JS API idempotently.

- Isolate::SetBeforeWasmMemoryDiscardCallback is consulted before the
  physical release. Releasing costs page-table work (a TLB shootdown
  IPI to every core running a thread of the process), imposed at
  whatever rate the guest calls the instruction; returning false
  declines the release and zeroes the range instead, which still
  satisfies memory.discard semantics and moves the cost onto the
  caller as CPU, so an embedder can rate limit the shootdowns.

Bug: v8:13787

